From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:10:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] configd: TX_SIZE() sign-extends on a 64-bit build, crashing
 multi-value updates

`svc.configd` dies of SIGSEGV importing any manifest whose `<dependency>`
holds more than one `<service_fmri>`. One FMRI is fine; two are fatal.

`object_tx_commit()` walks the values of a transaction command with

	v = (uint32_t *)((caddr_t)str + TX_SIZE(*v));

`TX_SIZE(x)` is `P2ROUNDUP((x), sizeof (uint32_t))`
(common/svc/repcache_protocol.h), and `P2ROUNDUP(x, align)` is
`(-(-(x) & -(align)))` (sys/sysmacros.h). `*v` is a `uint32_t`, so `-(x)` is
evaluated in 32 bits and then ZERO-extended to 64 before being masked against
a 64-bit `-(align)`; the closing negation therefore lands in the upper word:

	TX_SIZE((uint32_t)27) = 0xffffffff0000001c
	TX_SIZE((size_t)27)   = 0x000000000000001c

Here the result feeds straight into pointer arithmetic, so `v` ends up exactly
2^32 below where it belongs. The fault surfaces further along, in
`backend_tx_run_update()` -> sqlite's `vxprintf()`, reading the mangled string
pointer:

	client_switcher -> tx_commit -> rc_tx_commit -> object_tx_commit
	  -> tx_process_cmds -> backend_tx_run_update
	  -> sqlite_exec_vprintf -> sqlite_vmprintf -> base_vprintf -> vxprintf

with a fault address exactly 2^32 low. The line is only reached from the
SECOND iteration of the value loop, which is why the number of FMRIs decides
it.

The other `TX_SIZE()` call sites -- object.c and rc_node.c -- all assign the
result back into a 32-bit variable, which truncates the damage away. This one
does not.

Not reachable as illumos ships today, because `cmd/svc/configd` is still built
32-bit, where `size_t` is 32 bits and both halves agree. It bites immediately
on a 64-bit configd.

Fixed at the call site rather than in `P2ROUNDUP()`. The macro is arguably
hazardous for any caller whose operand is narrower than `align`, but changing
it reaches the whole tree, including the kernel; that is a separate and much
larger conversation.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5)
---
 usr/src/cmd/svc/configd/object.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/usr/src/cmd/svc/configd/object.c b/usr/src/cmd/svc/configd/object.c
index 6f76cd20b3..c7f1dfe601 100644
--- a/usr/src/cmd/svc/configd/object.c
+++ b/usr/src/cmd/svc/configd/object.c
@@ -320,8 +320,32 @@ tx_process_cmds(tx_commit_data_t *data)
 				if (r != REP_PROTOCOL_SUCCESS)
 					break;
 
+				/*
+				 * The (size_t) cast is load-bearing on a
+				 * 64-bit configd.  TX_SIZE() is
+				 * P2ROUNDUP(x, sizeof (uint32_t)), and
+				 * P2ROUNDUP() is (-(-(x) & -(align))): with
+				 * a uint32_t x, -(x) is evaluated in 32 bits
+				 * and then ZERO-extended before being masked
+				 * against a 64-bit -(align), so the closing
+				 * negation lands in the upper word --
+				 * TX_SIZE((uint32_t)27) is 0xffffffff0000001c
+				 * rather than 0x1c.  Here that feeds straight
+				 * into pointer arithmetic, putting `v' exactly
+				 * 2^32 below where it belongs and killing
+				 * configd with SIGSEGV.
+				 *
+				 * The other TX_SIZE() call sites assign the
+				 * result back into a 32-bit variable, which
+				 * truncates the damage away; this one does
+				 * not.  It is reached only from the second
+				 * iteration of this loop, so a dependency
+				 * with one service_fmri is fine and one with
+				 * two is fatal.
+				 */
 				/*LINTED alignment*/
-				v = (uint32_t *)((caddr_t)str + TX_SIZE(*v));
+				v = (uint32_t *)((caddr_t)str +
+				    TX_SIZE((size_t)*v));
 			}
 		}
 		if (r != REP_PROTOCOL_SUCCESS)
